WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.962
Welcome to Module 4 of Global Secure
Access as the Identity-Aware Network:

2
00:00:04.962 --> 00:00:08.263
private applications and
administrative paths.

3
00:00:08.263 --> 00:00:13.612
A V P N gives a user an address on the
network and lets the network decide the rest.

4
00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:16.983
Private Access gives each
application its own path:

5
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:21.763
its own hosts and ports, its own assigned
group, its own Conditional Access.

6
00:00:21.763 --> 00:00:27.464
This module covers Steps 6 and 10 of the
Global Secure Access implementation guide,

7
00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:32.120
and takes about fifty minutes, plus
a lab in your own test tenant.

8
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.020
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.358
A V P N address pool gives a
user an address on the network.

10
00:00:41.358 --> 00:00:43.647
If that user or device is compromised,

11
00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:48.669
the attacker can discover and move laterally
across everything the pool reaches.

12
00:00:48.669 --> 00:00:53.921
Private Access replaces the pool with
one path per application: its own hosts,

13
00:00:53.921 --> 00:00:58.105
ports and protocol, its own
assigned group, never individuals,

14
00:00:58.105 --> 00:01:01.633
and its own Conditional
Access, in report-only first.

15
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:06.320
A compromised user or device can reach
what it was assigned, and nothing else.

16
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.207
Then the module does the same for
the paths an attacker wants most:

17
00:01:10.207 --> 00:01:14.815
administrative remote desktop, behind
phishing-resistant authentication,

18
00:01:14.815 --> 00:01:18.620
and domain controllers, behind
the Private Access sensor.

19
00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.283
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

20
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:29.232
You'll explain why Quick Access is a
bridge that should shrink to nothing.

21
00:01:29.232 --> 00:01:33.410
You'll build a Private Access
application: bound to a connector group,

22
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:38.394
with only the segments it uses, a group
assignment, and a Conditional Access policy.

23
00:01:38.394 --> 00:01:43.031
You'll say where connector groups go,
including for applications in A W S,

24
00:01:43.031 --> 00:01:46.095
Google Cloud and Oracle
Cloud Infrastructure.

25
00:01:46.095 --> 00:01:48.345
You'll put administrative hosts behind the

26
00:01:48.345 --> 00:01:50.783
phishing-resistant
authentication strength.

27
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:54.874
And you'll describe the domain
controller sensor's audit-first order,

28
00:01:54.874 --> 00:01:56.382
and run Learn's klist test.

29
00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:59.294
You need Module 2; Module
3 isn't needed here,

30
00:01:59.294 --> 00:02:04.369
because the compliant network check
doesn't apply to Private Access apps.

31
00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:08.583
Read Network and Transport,
chapter five, on Private Access.

32
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:12.110
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production.

33
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:16.685
Unit 1.

34
00:02:16.685 --> 00:02:20.271
Network and Transport, chapter
five, holds the design:

35
00:02:20.271 --> 00:02:25.405
Quick Access as the bridge from the V P
N, application discovery to find what's

36
00:02:25.405 --> 00:02:28.421
actually used, and then
per-app applications,

37
00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:31.110
each with its own assignment and policy.

38
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.373
Learn's Graph tutorial is the build.

39
00:02:33.373 --> 00:02:35.866
Create the app from the custom application

40
00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:38.222
template and mark it a Private Access app.

41
00:02:38.222 --> 00:02:40.098
Bind it to one connector group.

42
00:02:40.098 --> 00:02:44.835
Add only the segments it uses:
one host, its ports, its protocol.

43
00:02:44.835 --> 00:02:47.259
Assign a group, with the app's User role;

44
00:02:47.259 --> 00:02:50.622
not individuals, and nested
groups aren't supported.

45
00:02:50.622 --> 00:02:55.862
Then put Conditional Access on it, in
report-only first, following Learn's example:

46
00:02:55.862 --> 00:03:00.397
multifactor authentication and a
compliant or hybrid-joined device,

47
00:03:00.397 --> 00:03:02.509
with emergency access excluded.

48
00:03:02.509 --> 00:03:05.785
Learn's rules: segments may
not overlap between apps,

49
00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:10.660
except that a per-app segment overrides
an overlapping Quick Access segment.

50
00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.747
Clients take up to fifteen
minutes to pick up a change.

51
00:03:13.747 --> 00:03:19.280
And Microsoft advises against an M F
A sign-in frequency on Quick Access,

52
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.540
because D N S queries trigger prompts.

53
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.665
Where do connectors go?

54
00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:30.425
They're outbound only,
with no inbound ports,

55
00:03:30.425 --> 00:03:35.026
they run on the program's server standard
of Windows Server 2019 or later,

56
00:03:35.026 --> 00:03:37.249
and they come at least two to a group,

57
00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:40.853
so a patch or an outage doesn't
take the applications down.

58
00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.041
Now the clouds.

59
00:03:42.041 --> 00:03:47.151
Learn describes Private Access as reaching
private apps across hybrid and multicloud

60
00:03:47.151 --> 00:03:49.812
environments, through
connectors on Windows

61
00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:52.753
Server hosts with line of
sight to the application.

62
00:03:52.753 --> 00:03:57.827
So an application in A W S, Google Cloud,
or Oracle Cloud Infrastructure gets

63
00:03:57.827 --> 00:04:02.902
a connector group in that cloud's network,
exactly as an on-premises one does.

64
00:04:02.902 --> 00:04:07.429
But Learn has no connector procedure
specific to any of those three clouds,

65
00:04:07.429 --> 00:04:09.764
so the guide marks it not yet specified,

66
00:04:09.764 --> 00:04:13.700
and it doesn't compare those
providers' own access services.

67
00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.212
How do you know it's least privilege?

68
00:04:19.212 --> 00:04:23.904
The guide's Validate for Step 6: from a
pilot device off the corporate network,

69
00:04:23.904 --> 00:04:28.670
reach the application on its port, and
confirm that a port outside the segment

70
00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:33.287
fails; the client's Policy tester shows
which rule handles the destination.

71
00:04:33.287 --> 00:04:37.252
The app's policy runs in
report-only for five business days,

72
00:04:37.252 --> 00:04:40.225
read in the sign-in log's
report-only results.

73
00:04:40.225 --> 00:04:43.617
Enforcement sets the policy
on, adds ring groups,

74
00:04:43.617 --> 00:04:48.875
and then turns off the V P N route for
that application, which Module 6 covers.

75
00:04:48.875 --> 00:04:54.145
Rollback removes the group's assignment,
or puts the policy back in report-only,

76
00:04:54.145 --> 00:04:57.063
and the V P N route stays
until the wave holds.

77
00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:01.387
On day two, the application owner
reviews the assignment each quarter,

78
00:05:01.387 --> 00:05:05.637
as an access review, and the platform
owner keeps at least two healthy,

79
00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:07.501
current connectors per group.

80
00:05:07.501 --> 00:05:12.154
The metric is the Quick Access
residual: segments left in Quick Access,

81
00:05:12.154 --> 00:05:14.963
and apps without an
assignment or a policy.

82
00:05:14.963 --> 00:05:17.820
The starting target for both is zero.

83
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.495
Unit 2 is administrative remote desktop.

84
00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:31.083
Administrative R D P, S S H and S M B
are the paths an attacker wants most.

85
00:05:31.083 --> 00:05:35.753
Private Access makes each a per-app
application with its own assignment and its own

86
00:05:35.753 --> 00:05:39.069
Conditional Access, so an
administrator authenticates to

87
00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:42.657
Entra with a phishing-resistant
method before the tunnel opens.

88
00:05:42.657 --> 00:05:47.703
Publish administrative hosts as their own
applications, separate from user apps:

89
00:05:47.703 --> 00:05:52.303
one app per tier or host group, with the
host on port thirty-three eighty-nine,

90
00:05:52.303 --> 00:05:56.607
assigned only to the privileged
group whose pim activation grants it.

91
00:05:56.607 --> 00:06:01.896
The policy requires the built-in
phishing-resistant authentication strength,

92
00:06:01.896 --> 00:06:06.144
whose I D ends in four, and a
compliant device, in report-only first.

93
00:06:06.144 --> 00:06:11.832
Enforcement ends the old path: the policy
goes on, and then direct R D P is removed

94
00:06:11.832 --> 00:06:17.360
from the V P N and from the server
firewall, except from the connector I P's.

95
00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:23.498
Unit 3 is domain controllers.

96
00:06:23.498 --> 00:06:28.610
The program retires its domain controllers;
until then, they're Tier zero.

97
00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.296
Learn's procedure: publish the
D C's on T C P eighty-eight,

98
00:06:32.296 --> 00:06:37.323
in Quick Access or an app, and name
the service principal names to protect.

99
00:06:37.323 --> 00:06:41.672
Open inbound T C P thirteen
thirty-seven on the D C's.

100
00:06:41.672 --> 00:06:46.523
Install one sensor per D C, silently,
and register it with a token.

101
00:06:46.523 --> 00:06:48.831
The sensor installs in Audit mode,

102
00:06:48.831 --> 00:06:52.460
so you read its event log
before anything is enforced.

103
00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:56.806
Learn's advice is to test with
application S P N's first,

104
00:06:56.806 --> 00:07:01.744
and protect the D C's own S P N
only later, to avoid admin lockout,

105
00:07:01.744 --> 00:07:05.398
and to assess N T L M use
before restricting it.

106
00:07:05.398 --> 00:07:09.783
The rollback is the sensor's
break-glass mode, from the admin center,

107
00:07:09.783 --> 00:07:13.372
or a registry value set to
one and the sensor restarted.

108
00:07:13.372 --> 00:07:16.138
And the registration
token is a credential:

109
00:07:16.138 --> 00:07:21.580
acquire it on a privileged access workstation,
use it once, and clear the session.

110
00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:30.155
The lab runs in a test tenant, never
production, and the authors haven't run it.

111
00:07:30.155 --> 00:07:33.531
You need Module 2's lab done,
a Private Access test license,

112
00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:37.984
a test connector group with two connectors
on Windows Server 2019 or later,

113
00:07:37.984 --> 00:07:42.581
a test application server and a test
administrative host reachable from the

114
00:07:42.581 --> 00:07:45.310
connectors, and a
privileged test group whose

115
00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.542
members have phishing-resistant
methods registered.

116
00:07:48.542 --> 00:07:51.565
Step one lists the connector
groups and connectors;

117
00:07:51.565 --> 00:07:56.204
the guide's resilience metric wants at
least two active connectors in the group.

118
00:07:56.204 --> 00:07:59.091
Step two creates the
application from the custom

119
00:07:59.091 --> 00:08:01.555
template and marks it
a Private Access app.

120
00:08:01.555 --> 00:08:05.809
Step three binds it to the connector
group and adds one segment:

121
00:08:05.809 --> 00:08:08.835
the application's host
name, its ports, and T C P.

122
00:08:08.835 --> 00:08:12.680
Step four assigns the pilot
group with the app's User role.

123
00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:18.070
And step five puts the device and
M F A policy on it, in report-only.

124
00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.437
Step six publishes the
administrative host the same way,

125
00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:29.670
on thirty-three eighty-nine, assigned
only to the privileged group.

126
00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:33.617
Step seven creates the phishing-resistant
policy in report-only,

127
00:08:33.617 --> 00:08:37.783
then enables the private forwarding
profile and assigns the groups.

128
00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:42.046
Step eight tests the path from the
pilot device off the corporate network,

129
00:08:42.046 --> 00:08:45.960
about fifteen minutes after the
last change: the application's port

130
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.594
should be reachable, and a port
outside the segment should fail.

131
00:08:49.594 --> 00:08:53.595
The client's Policy tester shows
which rule handles the destination.

132
00:08:53.595 --> 00:08:56.995
Step nine reads the private
sessions in the workspace.

133
00:08:56.995 --> 00:09:00.715
Step ten connects with remote
desktop as a privileged user,

134
00:09:00.715 --> 00:09:04.208
and the sign-in log shows the
policy's report-only result.

135
00:09:04.208 --> 00:09:09.758
Step eleven reads both policies' report-only
results; leave them in report-only.

136
00:09:09.758 --> 00:09:15.294
And step twelve is optional, where a test
domain has the sensor: Learn's klist test.

137
00:09:15.294 --> 00:09:18.670
With client and sensor
off, the ticket is issued.

138
00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:22.645
With the sensor on and the
client off, S M B is blocked.

139
00:09:22.645 --> 00:09:27.350
With both on, the ticket is issued
after the Conditional Access prompt.

140
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.711
The federal requirement, from NIST special

141
00:09:33.711 --> 00:09:36.975
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

142
00:09:36.975 --> 00:09:41.291
Private applications support A
C three, A C six, A C seventeen,

143
00:09:41.291 --> 00:09:47.281
and its enhancement three: each application
reachable only by its assigned group,

144
00:09:47.281 --> 00:09:50.100
on its ports, under Conditional Access.

145
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.403
The evidence is the app, segment,
assignment and policy exports,

146
00:09:54.403 --> 00:09:59.700
the reachable and unreachable port tests,
and the Quick Access residual list.

147
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:05.406
Administrative paths support I A two
enhancements one and two, A C six enhancement

148
00:10:05.406 --> 00:10:11.112
nine, and A C seventeen: phishing-resistant
authentication and a compliant device.

149
00:10:11.112 --> 00:10:14.936
The evidence is the app and
policy exports, the sensor mode,

150
00:10:14.936 --> 00:10:18.362
the klist test record, and
the break-glass rehearsal.

151
00:10:18.362 --> 00:10:23.328
The program's mapping lines Step 6 up
with O M B memo M twenty-two oh nine's

152
00:10:23.328 --> 00:10:27.952
application access without a V P N,
and tick three point oh's Remote

153
00:10:27.952 --> 00:10:32.662
User Pattern one, and Step 10 with
the memo's phishing-resistant M F A.

154
00:10:32.662 --> 00:10:36.820
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

155
00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.545
Check yourself with two questions.

156
00:10:42.545 --> 00:10:46.470
First: an application runs in
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure.

157
00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:47.920
How does the guide reach it?

158
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.870
The answer is a: through a connector
group in that cloud's network,

159
00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:55.670
with line of sight to the application,
as for an on-premises one.

160
00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.646
Second: in what order does
Learn protect service

161
00:10:58.646 --> 00:11:01.845
principal names with the
domain controller sensor?

162
00:11:01.845 --> 00:11:05.486
The answer is b:
application S P N's first,

163
00:11:05.486 --> 00:11:09.582
the D C's own S P N later,
to avoid admin lockout.

164
00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:14.850
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

165
00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:19.425
To sum up.

166
00:11:19.425 --> 00:11:24.686
You published one application and one
administrative host as their own Private

167
00:11:24.686 --> 00:11:29.712
Access apps, each with only its segments,
its own group, and a report-only policy.

168
00:11:29.712 --> 00:11:34.655
You tested the reachable and refused
ports, read the private sessions and

169
00:11:34.655 --> 00:11:39.274
the policies' results, and, where a
sensor exists, ran Learn's klist test.

170
00:11:39.274 --> 00:11:43.217
The evidence an I S S O keeps from
this module: the app, segment,

171
00:11:43.217 --> 00:11:48.608
assignment and policy exports; the port
test record and the Policy tester result;

172
00:11:48.608 --> 00:11:53.838
the private session query; the administrative
sign-in record; the klist record;

173
00:11:53.838 --> 00:11:58.263
and the Quick Access residual list,
which should shrink to nothing.

174
00:11:58.263 --> 00:12:02.383
Next is Module 5, Internet
Access and T L S inspection,

175
00:12:02.383 --> 00:12:04.940
which come last in the run order.
